{
  "dom_id": "step-q38066",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) above under the action of $T?$",
  "worked_solution": "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/85ff90a404a22310659fadd3a00c58490c9745d443b180d6b509b40a5497376b.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
